 The dialog also has a page box, where a person types which pages of the
 document to print.
+
+```js
+import { parseRanges } from "./src/parse-ranges.mjs";
+
+parseRanges("1-3,5", 10); // [1, 2, 3, 5]
+parseRanges("7", 10);     // [7]
+```
+
+`parseRanges(text, pageCount)` reads the page box and gives the page numbers to
+print as an array. Pages are numbered from 1 to `pageCount`. The parts of the
+text are separated by commas, and a part is one page or a range written
+`first-last`; spaces around a page, a comma or a hyphen are ignored. The pages
+come back in ascending order, each one once, however they were typed.
+
+A `text` that is not a string throws a `TypeError`, and so does a `pageCount`
+that is not a positive integer. A text that cannot be read as pages of the
+document throws a `RangeError`: a page `0` or a page past the last one, a word,
+a part that is neither a page nor a range, an empty text or an empty part, or a
+range whose first page comes after its last, such as `5-3`.
